{"results":[{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"SummitView Power BI gateway monitoring commercial features","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://www.summitview.app/features/gateway-monitoring","title":"Power BI Gateway Monitoring | SummitView","domain":"www.summitview.app","summary":"# Spot personal gateways and connectivity risks before they cause failures.\n- Find every personal gateway hiding in your tenant\n- Know the instant a gateway goes offline\n- See which datasets break when a gateway fails\n14-day free trial · No credit card · 5-minute setup\n...\nWith SummitView\n- Every gateway and connection cataloged, including personal ones\n- Online/offline monitoring with version tracking\n- Dependency mapping shows which datasets are at risk\n## See it in action\n...\n### Key capabilities\n#### Personal Gateway Flagging\nIdentify unmanaged gateways tied to individual machines and users.\n#### Status Monitoring\nTrack online/offline status and alert when gateways go down.\n#### Version Tracking\nFlag outdated gateway versions that pose compatibility and security risks.\n#### Dependency Mapping\nSee which datasets depend on which gateways for impact analysis.\n## What you get in SummitView\nTenant-wide gateway inventory\nenterprise and personal\nOnline/offline status monitoring with alerting\nPersonal gateway connection flagging with governance risk indicators\nData source count and type tracking per gateway\nVersion tracking with outdated-version warnings\nDependency mapping\nsee which datasets depend on which gateways\nAffected-dataset context in every alert\nknow the blast radius instantly\n## Up and running in minutes\n...\n1\nConnect via Cloud Connect or the optional agent — SummitView enumerates all gateways and data sources automatically.\n2\nGateway health, version, and type are monitored continuously.\n3\nPersonal gateways and offline gateways trigger alerts with affected-dataset context.\n...\n## Works with every license tier\nNo matter your Power BI license, gateway monitoring is included.\nPower BI Pro\nCore monitoring, usage tracking, alerting\nPremium Per User\nAll Pro features + per-table timing, paginated reports\nMicrosoft Fabric\nAll PPU features + capacity metrics, throttling detection\nGateway Monitoring works with all Power BI license types.\nIt monitors gateways at the tenant level — both enterprise gateways managed by IT and personal gateways installed by individual users.\n...\nA single health score for your Power BI tenant, updated daily with trends and actionable recommendations.\n...\nTrack every semantic model refresh across your tenant — failures, slow refreshes, and missing refreshes in real time.\n...\nStart your 14-day free trial.\nNo credit card required.\nSet up in 5 minutes.\n$299/mo per tenant\nUnlimited users\nAll features included","date":"2026-02-01"},{"url":"https://www.summitview.app","title":"SummitView - Complete Power BI Visibility","domain":"www.summitview.app","summary":"Track every refresh.\nDetect failures before users report them.\nKeep unlimited usage history.\nAll from one read-only dashboard.\nNo credit card required · 14 days full access · Set up in 5 minutes\n...\nBring your own AI keyUnlimited historyMetadata onlyWindows SSO5 min setup\n...\nSummitView is built for the teams responsible for keeping Power BI running.\n### Power BI Admins\nReal-time visibility into refreshes, gateways, capacity, and usage across every workspace.\n- Alerted to failures before users notice\n- Gateway and capacity monitoring in one view\n- Per-table timing to isolate bottlenecks\nView overview\n### BI Teams\nUsage analytics, refresh performance, and governance tools to prioritize what matters.\n- Usage history beyond 30 days\n- Row count anomaly detection\n- Governance tagging and asset lifecycle\nView overview\n### Power BI Architects\nPerformance baselines, capacity planning, and operational governance for platform decisions.\n- Data-driven capacity planning\n- Migration readiness assessment\n- Webhook integration with incident tools\nView overview\n...\n## Everything you need for Power BI observability\nStop flying blind.\nGet the visibility your Power BI environment deserves.\n### Monitor & Alert\nKnow before your users do\n- Refresh tracking with per-table timing (PPU/Fabric)\n- Historical average comparison — see % faster/slower per refresh\n- Missing refresh detection — alert when expected refreshes don't run\n- Row count anomaly detection across all datasets\n- Teams, Slack, and email alerts with smart cooldowns\n### Analyze & Optimize\nTurn data into decisions\n- Usage analytics with unlimited history (not just 30 days)\n- Health scoring for datasets, workspaces, and tenant overall\n- Capacity insights — CPU, memory, and throttling detection\n- AI-powered recommendations using your own API key (BYOK)\n### Govern & Share\nStructure and control at scale\n- Complete inventory of workspaces, datasets, and reports\n- Governance editor — tag criticality, owners, and lifecycle\n- Scheduling and collision detection across all datasets\n- Consumer portal — a branded catalog of published reports\n- Access request workflow for gated reports\nKey differentiator\n...\nSummitView retains your usage history **forever** — giving you real trend analysis and the data to make informed decisions.\n...\nSummitView learns your refresh schedules and alerts you the moment an expected refresh doesn't appear — before your users open a report with yesterday's data.\nIt also detects **anomalies automatically**: a refresh that takes 3x longer than its historical average, or a table that suddenly drops 34% of its rows.\nYou'll know immediately — not when a VP asks why the numbers look wrong.\n...\nWhen a refresh takes twice as long as usual, Power BI just tells you it was slow.\nSummitView shows you **exactly which table** is the bottleneck — with **% faster/slower badges** compared to historical averages so you can see if it's trending worse over time.\nAvailable for PPU and Fabric workspaces.\nModel build time is calculated separately from table refreshes, giving you a complete performance breakdown.\n...\n|Capability|Power BI Native|SummitView|\n|--|--|--|\n|Usage history retention|30 days|Forever|\n|Refresh vs. historical average| | |\n|Per-table refresh timing| | |\n...\n|Historical trend analysis|Limited|Unlimited|\n...\nSummitView is observability for Power BI.\n## One price.\nFull Power BI visibility.\nUnlimited users.\nUnlimited workspaces.\nPer tenant.\nAll features included — no feature tiers, no per-user pricing, no usage penalties.\nMonthlyAnnual2 months free\n...\n### Pro\n$2,999/year\nper Power BI tenant\n~$250/mo — 2 months free\n- Unlimited workspaces & datasets\n- Unlimited users\n- Unlimited history retention\n- Per-table refresh timing (PPU/Fabric)\n- Real-time anomaly detection\n- Usage analytics & inventory\n- Gateway & capacity monitoring\n- Teams, Slack & email alerts\n- AI analysis (BYOK)\n- Cloud Connect + Agent supported\n14 days free • No credit card required\n### Enterprise\nCustom\nfor procurement, security & multi-tenant\nEverything in Pro, plus:\n- Security review support\n- Invoice / PO available\n- Optional SLA\n- Multiple Power BI tenants\n- SSO / SAML integration\n- On-premise deployment option\n- Dedicated support\n...\nBilled annually.\nMonth-to-month available.\nMost teams choose annual for continuous monitoring.\n...\nSummitView works with Pro, PPU, Premium, and Fabric.\nPer-table timing requires PPU/Premium/Fabric.\n...\nYes.\nAnnual is the default ($2,999/yr — 2 months free), but month-to-month billing is available at $299/mo.\n...\nSet up in minutes.\nFull access for 14 days.\nNo credit card required.\nFrom $2,999/year • Month-to-month available at $299/mo"},{"url":"https://www.summitview.app/power-bi-monitoring-tools","title":"How to Evaluate Power BI...","domain":"www.summitview.app","summary":"**Look for:** Proactive alerting, unlimited history, capacity monitoring, multi-channel notifications, and a security model that doesn't require write access.\n...\nSummitView is a Power BI observability platform built specifically for admins and architects who need proactive visibility across their tenant.\n### Key design principles\n- **Metadata-only, read-only:** SummitView collects metadata about refreshes, usage, and workspace structure.\nIt never accesses actual report data or business information.\nSee our security model.\n- **Proactive alerting with baselines:** Automatic detection of failed refreshes, missing refreshes, slow refreshes, and row count anomalies.\nAlerts route to email, Teams, Slack, or webhooks.\n- **Unlimited usage history:** Power BI retains activity events for 30 days.\nSummitView retains them indefinitely, enabling long-term usage analytics and trend analysis.\n- **Works across Pro, PPU, and Fabric:** No feature lockouts by license tier.\nPPU and Fabric workspaces get additional depth like per-table refresh timing and capacity monitoring.\n- **Gateway and inventory visibility:** Monitor gateway health, flag personal connections, and maintain a complete workspace inventory.\nSummitView uses Cloud Connect (service principal) as its primary data source—no agent installation required.\nAn optional Windows agent provides enhanced features like per-table refresh timing for PPU/Fabric environments.\n...\nSummitView gives Power BI admins and architects proactive visibility into refresh health, data quality, usage trends, and capacity—across every workspace and license tier.","date":"2026-02-19"},{"url":"https://www.summitview.app/power-bi-observability","title":"Power BI Observability: Complete Platform Visibility - SummitView","domain":"www.summitview.app","summary":"## How SummitView Helps\nSummitView provides complete Power BI observability:\n- **Unified dashboard** across all monitoring domains\n- **Unlimited history** for trending and analysis\n- **Correlation engine** connecting issues to causes\n- **Proactive alerts** based on patterns\n## FAQ\n...\nMonitoring tracks predefined metrics and alerts on known conditions.\nObservability provides the tools to investigate any question about system behavior—including issues you didn't anticipate.\n...\nAt minimum, 90 days for trend analysis.\nIdeally, 12+ months to capture seasonal patterns, year-over-year comparisons, and demonstrate long-term improvement.\n...\nBegin with the highest-impact areas: refresh monitoring (reliability), usage analytics (adoption), and capacity monitoring (performance).\nExpand from there based on your specific needs.","date":"2025-01-18"},{"url":"https://www.summitview.app/features","title":"Features | SummitView - Complete Power BI Visibility","domain":"www.summitview.app","summary":"Complete Power BI Monitoring Platform\n...\nRefreshes failing at 3 AM.\nReports nobody uses.\nGateways nobody owns.\nSummitView surfaces what Power BI hides.\n14-day free trial — no credit card\n...\nMetadata only — never your data\n∞\nUsage history retained\nvs 30 days in Power BI\nPer-table\nRefresh timing\nPPU & Fabric\n8\nAlert types\nRefresh, anomaly, capacity & more\n4\nNotification channels\nTeams, Slack, email, webhooks\nRefresh Monitoring\n### Never find out about a failed refresh from an end user again.\nTrack every semantic model refresh across your entire tenant in real time.\nWhen a refresh fails, see the error message, which table was affected, and a recommended fix — not just a generic notification.\nFor PPU and Fabric, get per-table timing with historical comparison, model build time, and faster/slower badges.\n- Pinpoint exactly which table caused a slowdown\n- Fix refresh issues before users notice\n- Track refresh trends across months, not just 30 days\nSee refresh risks instantly\nRefresh Monitoring\n...\n### Answer 'who uses what' with months of data, not just the last 30 days.\nPower BI deletes usage data after 30 days.\nSummitView captures events daily and stores them indefinitely — giving you months of history to analyze adoption, identify unused reports, and make data-driven license optimization decisions.\n- Break through Power BI’s 30-day usage data limit\n- Find unused reports costing you licenses every month\n- Track adoption trends to prove BI investment ROI\nSee who actually uses your reports\n...\nCatch the problems Power BI's refresh status misses.\nSummitView tracks row counts, durations, and data patterns after each refresh, flagging deviations from historical baselines — even when the refresh shows 'Completed.'\n- Catch silent data corruption instantly\n- Stop 'successful' refreshes that load no data\n- Know which issues to fix first, automatically\nStart tracking anomalies\n...\nA composite health score for your Power BI tenant, broken into five dimensions and updated daily.\nIt surfaces top risks in priority order, compares trends against prior periods, and translates scores into concrete recommended actions — so you know exactly what needs attention and why.\n- One score tells you if your tenant needs attention today\n- Top risks ranked by severity with recommended fixes\n- Week-over-week trends prove your environment is improving\nKnow your tenant health in 30 seconds\n...\nEvery feature included.\nNo add-ons.\nNo upgrades.\n### Scheduling\nVisualize refresh schedules across your tenant with collision detection and off-peak optimization.\n### Inventory\nA complete catalog of every workspace, dataset, report, and gateway across your Power BI tenant.\n### Governance\nTag, classify, and track ownership of every Power BI asset with a governance editor and automated hygiene scoring.\n### Capacity\nMonitor CPU, memory, and throttling for Fabric and Premium capacities to prevent performance degradation.\n### Alerting\nMulti-channel alerts with smart cooldowns, plain-English context, and actionable fix suggestions.\n### Gateway Monitoring\nTrack gateway health, flag personal gateway connections, and identify single points of failure in your data infrastructure.\n### AI Insights\nAI-powered recommendations and summaries using your own API key.\nBYOK, optional, and off by default.\n### Report Portal\nA branded, consumer-facing catalog for published Power BI reports with search, filtering, and gated access requests.\n...\n## One plan.\nEverything included.\n$299/month per tenant.\nUnlimited users.\nUnlimited workspaces.\nNo per-seat pricing.\nNo feature gates.\n...\nNo. SummitView collects metadata only — refresh status, timing, row counts, usage events, and workspace information.\nIt never accesses the data inside your semantic models, reports, or dashboards.\nSummitView works with Power BI Pro, Premium Per User (PPU), Premium capacity, and Microsoft Fabric.\nSome features like per-table refresh timing require PPU or Fabric.\nCore monitoring works on all license types.\n...\nYes.\nSummitView offers a 14-day free trial with full access to all features.\nNo credit card required.\nSummitView Pro is $299/month or $2,999/year per tenant.\nUnlimited users, unlimited workspaces, all features included.\nNo per-user pricing.\nYes.\nCloud Connect uses a service principal to collect data without any software installation.\nThe agent provides additional capabilities like per-table timing and reliable Pro workspace refresh status."},{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance","title":"Monitor and optimize on-premises data gateway performance","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"To monitor performance, gateway admins have traditionally depended on manually monitoring performance counters through the Windows Performance Monitor tool.\nWe now offer additional query logging and a Gateway Performance PBI template file to visualize the results.\nThis feature provides new insights into gateway usage.\nYou can use it to troubleshoot slow-performing queries.\nNote\nThis feature is currently available only for the on-premises data gateway in the standard mode.\nIt's not available for the personal mode.\n...\nGateway diagnostics doesn't capture diagnostics directly related to the (virtual) machine and its network, like bandwidth or latency.\n...\nThis feature is now turned on by default.\nNote\n- Currently, queries from premium capacity to the gateway are sometimes missed in this logging.\nWe are actively working on fixing this issue.\n- Currently, Power BI paginated report queries aren't logged using this tool.\n...\n- **ReportFileCount**: Determines the number of log files of each kind to retain.\nThe default value is 10.\n...\nThe default value is 104,857,600.\n...\nThe default value is 5.\n...\nThe default value is 5.\n...\nWhen you turn on this feature, four new log files are created:\n- The Query Execution Report\n- The Query Start Report\n- The Query Execution Aggregation Report\n- The System Counter Aggregation Report\n...\nNow, you can visualize the data that's in the log files.\n1. Download the Gateway Performance PBI template, and open it by using Power BI Desktop.\n2. In the dialog box that opens, check that the folder path matches the value in **ReportFilePath**.\n3. Select **Load**, and the template file starts loading the data from your log files.\nAll visuals are populated by using the data in the reports.\n4. Optionally, save this file as a PBIX, and publish it to your service for automatic refreshes.\nTo learn more, go to Publish semantic models and reports from Power BI Desktop.\n...\nTo do this, in the on-premises data gateway app select **Diagnostics** > **Additional logging**.\n...\n1. In the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes** setting to **True**, and then save.\n```\n<setting name=\"StreamBeforeRequestCompletes\" serializeAs=\"String\">\n<value>True</value>\n</setting>\n```\n2. In **On-premises data gateway** > **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2025-06-12"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Minceddata Power BI gateway monitoring Fabric blog 2023","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://www.minceddata.info/2023/10/16/power-bi-gateway-monitoring-and-more-using-microsoft-fabric/","title":"Power BI Gateway monitoring and more using Microsoft Fabric","domain":"www.minceddata.info","summary":"This article describes a solution built with Microsoft Fabric.\nIt’s not necessary to use Fabric, and it’s also not necessary to build a solution on your own.\nIf you want to track the well-being of your on-premises data gateways but do not want to build something, I recommend using the solution by Rui Romano you can find here: https://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\nI built this monitoring solution focusing on the well-being of the on-premises data gateway.\nI might extend this solution in the future, but for now, it’s about the availability of the on-premises data gateway and the data gateway connections.\n...\nI use Microsoft Fabric because I do not have to care about getting access to many different Azure resources.\nI only need a Microsoft Fabric-enabled workspace and a Service Principal.\nWhile the components of Microsoft Fabric are automatically available when using a Microsoft Fabric-enabled workspace, I have to take care of the Service Principal.\nHaving easy access to notebooks (for Python coding), pipelines (for orchestration and automation), a lakehouse (for data storage), and, of course, Power BI makes the promise of an integrated SaaS data analytics platform come true.\n...\nThese resources are the Power BI REST APIs and the Service Principal.\n...\nFor tracking the well-being of the on-premises data gateway(s), I use the REST API endpoint\n`gateways` described here: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/power-bi/gateways/get-gateways.\nLater, I will extend this solution to\nother REST APIs from the gateway group.\nI consider monitoring the Power BI environment a good idea, so I consider using the Power BI REST APIs essential.\n...\nGranting permissions to a service principal should always follow the concept of least privileges.\nFollowing this concept leads to the creation of multiple service principals.\nThis means you need documentation of the service principals you allow access to the APIs of your service - the Power BI Service.\n...\nThe following image shows how you add the Service Principal (not the security group, this is only for demonstration purposes) as an Admin to the on-premises data gateway(s) you want to monitor, the second resource that requires authorization next to the REST API.\n# Monitoring the availability of your on-premises data gateway using Microsoft Fabric\n...\nThis is the first time that the artifact I publicly share is not a pbix file but Python code.\nHere, you will find the notebook “tenantMonitoring_gateways.ipynb” (https://tommartens-my.sharepoint.com/:u:/g/personal/tom_minceddata_com/EaxaEdtqMo5FvUdSstAAuoEBmo2PUYj4kXdqEqLCjoFUhA?e=MrF5eU).\n...\nBasically, the current solution is simple: I have a Fabric-enabled workspace, a lakehouse, and a notebook, but the solution will grow during the next iterations.\nThe following image is showing the current state (October 2023) of my architecture:\nInside the lakehouse, I created a subfolder “GatewayInformation,” inside the files section.\nThis is the place where I store the result of a REST API endpoint request as a CSV file, of course, after some data transformations.\nI write the same dataframe (almost the same) that I have written as CSV also as a table in delta format using the vorder sorting.","date":"2023-10-16"},{"url":"https://www.minceddata.info/blog/microsoft-fabric/","title":"Microsoft Fabric","domain":"www.minceddata.info","summary":"Microsoft Fabric\n· 17. Oktober 2023\nPower BI Gateway monitoring and more using Microsoft Fabric\nNo matter what, when the Power BI on-premises gateways are not working as expected, data will not refresh, and direct query queries will not succeed.\nIt's almost simple to create a monitoring solution using Microsoft Fabric\nMehr lesen...","date":"2023-12-30"},{"url":"https://www.minceddata.info/2023/09/24/a-song-to-the-unnoticed-heroes-of-every-successful-power-bi-solution-the-on-premises-data-gateway-the-vnet-gateway-and-the-sharable-cloud-connection/","title":"A song to the unnoticed heroes of every successful Power BI ...","domain":"www.minceddata.info","summary":"- all data sources forming a dataset must leverage the same gateway cluster\n- if people are going to leave the team, make sure the team is aware of the data source names and does know the password of any data source involved\n- Ideas on how to manage your on-premises data gateways from a technical point of view with a touch of an organizational perspective This article will be about building clusters, aligning your gateway clusters with different workloads, like import vs. directquery, or aligning gateway clusters with different environments, like dev, test, and prod","date":"2023-09-24"},{"url":"https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Power-BI-Community-Blog/Power-BI-Gateway-Monitoring-amp-Administrating-Part-2/ba-p/750765","title":"Power BI: Gateway Monitoring & Administrating- Part 2","domain":"community.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"This is the second part where I'll explain how to create a report out of it and configure gateways & alerts and manage the alerts with the help of PowerPlatforms such as Microsoft Flow.\n...\nWe already have our data which we created from the previous article with that we are going to create some cool reports to manage and administrate the gateways.\nHere I've created 4 main reports but you can explore much with the data we already have.\n## Gateway Clustering Monitoring Report\nThis is monitoring report whic will tell the summary of how many gateways are Online/Offline.\nHere I've used the below filters and charts\n1. Image Slicer which will help you to filter **Active** and **InActive** gateways\n2. Environment Filter to filterout Production and Non-Production Gateways\n3. Cards to show to the Exact no of Active and InActive Gateways.\n4. Pie-Chart for the summary.\n5. Table to find the Gateway details such as Name ,Environment,MachineName and other details for the Gateways\n##\n## Gateway Cluster Report\nThis Report will basically tell the details of the Clusters and Gateways installed totally.\n**Clusters** : A group of gateway(s) installed on different machine for high- availability and load balancing\n**Gateway**: A individual on-premises gateway installed on a single machine\nIf you note the report I've used hierachy to enable the drill down to find how many gateways are configured for a particular cluster.\n## Gateway Report By Version\nThis report give you summary of what are the different versions of gateway currently being installed.\n...\n1. A Text File\n2. Gateways Cluster REST API\n3. Gateway REST API\nIn order to refresh our report we need a gateway, because our text file is located in the Server.So let's configure the gateway now.\nTricks: If you thought of skiping the the other 2 data source as those API (no need gateway to communicate) then you probabily wrong.\nIn order to refresh your report you need to add the other 2 data source with **Skip Test**\n**API 1**\n**API 2**\n****\nBy doing this will we are adding a dummy sources so that we can able to refresh our reports in PowerBI service.Actually we are going to provide the **Bearer Token ** dyamically from our Text document.\nSo we don't need to really care about this dummy source\nNow we can map the gateway to our dataset after that we are good to **Refresh** we can also configure the **scheduled refresh **\nHurray we have successfully completed our Report creation ,publishing and configured gateway for scheduled refresh.\nNow It's time to setup the alerts .For that we need to Pin our Gateway off-line card/tile to our Dashboard.\nFrom the Dashboard **Three Dots- > ManagerAlerts**\nCreate an Alert when the **Threashold Reach above 0, ** which means any on of the gateway went **offline**\nUnfortunately only you can able to receive this alert.\nBut with the help of Microsoft flow we can send these alerts to others also, let see how we can do that.\n# Configure PowerBI Alerts in Microsoft Flows\nLogin to flow.microsoft.com and from there you can choose the exsiting template called **Trigger a flow with PowerBI Data Driven Alert**\nFrom there choose the alerts which you created from the previous step\nNow Add the Email Action to sent the Email to the team\nFinally Add the Teams action to Post a message to the Teams Channel\nLet's Test this whether we are able to get the alerts\n...\nIn this series of article I just showed you how we can able to configure and administrate PowerBI on-premises Gateway by using PowerBI Gateway API's.\nThis is not only for Gateway API.\nYou can call any PowerBI API's to manage all our PowerBI components with-in PowerBI itself.\nYou also learned how to manage the alerts produced from PowerBI to sent across Microsoft Teams, Outlooks with the help of Flow.","date":"2019-08-07"},{"url":"https://community.powerbi.com/t5/Power-Query/Monitoring-On-Premises-Data-Gateway/td-p/56371","title":"Monitoring On-Premises Data Gateway - Microsoft Fabric Community","domain":"community.powerbi.com","summary":"The paper available for download on my blog describes the approach and related considerations:\n...\nGateway monitoring is part of my current project and I may write a new, updated paper or blog post in February of 2018.","date":"2019-06-17"},{"url":"https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Service/Intermittent-Issues-and-Monitoring-for-On-Premises-Data-Gateway/m-p/4279186","title":"Intermittent Issues and Monitoring for On-Premises Data Gateway in ...","domain":"community.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"For the purpose of monitoring gateway status, you can use Azure Logic Apps/Power automate for your purposes, you can refer to the following blog:\nMonitor your On-premises data gateway with Azure and Power BI – Part 1 – Gateway up-time – Data – Ma...Hi @razec18 - let's look at options for alerting, improving high availability, and troubleshooting potential causes of intermittent failures.\nAzure Monitor: Azure Monitor can be configured to alert you if there is an issue with the VM running your gateway.\nSet up a custom metric alert for the On-Premises Data Gateway service status on your VM:\nCreate a Log Analytics Workspace and install the Log Analytics Agent on your VM.\nConfigure the workspace to collect Windows Event logs related to gateway service restarts and network connectivity issues (e.g., event IDs related to “service stop” or “unexpected termination”).\nIn Azure Monitor, create alerts based on these logs and send notifications via email, SMS, or integrate with Azure Logic Apps to trigger automated responses.\nPower BI Gateway Monitoring: Use the Power BI Gateway Management Console to track the status of your gateway.\nFor more granular monitoring, you could use PowerShell scripts to periodically check the gateway status and send notifications if it goes offline.\nSince your gateway was stable until a specific time, several factors could have triggered the instability.\nHere are some possible root causes and diagnostic steps:\nNetwork Connectivity: Azure VMs sometimes experience connectivity issues if there are network configuration changes, updates, or maintenance in the Azure region.\nCheck Network Security Group (NSG) Rules: Ensure that no changes have been made to your NSG rules, which might impact the VM's ability to reach your on-premises environment.\nReview Firewall and Proxy Settings: Confirm that the firewall and any proxies in your environment are configured to allow the necessary traffic for the gateway.\nSome firewalls may intermittently block connections due to configured timeouts.\nGateway Logs: The On-Premises Data Gateway logs may provide insight into the cause of intermittent issues.\nYou can find logs in the Event Viewer under Applications and Services Logs > On-Premises Data Gateway Service.\nLook for warnings or errors around the time the issue started.\nGateway logs typically include errors like timeouts, network drops, or throttling issues.\nAzure VM Resource Health: Monitor the resource health of your Azure VM in the Azure portal.\nIf Azure detects any health issues with the underlying hardware or software, they will be reported here.\nEnsure your gateway software is the latest version, as updates often resolve stability and performance issues.\nScheduled Restarts: In cases where connectivity issues are frequent, consider scheduling regular restarts of the gateway service during non-peak hours.\nThis can help mitigate memory or resource leaks.\nBackup Configuration: If possible, back up your gateway configuration, which will allow for quicker recovery in case of a failure or reinstallation.\nHope these, strategies should improve your gateway’s resilience, and by combining alerts with high-availability clustering, you'll have greater monitoring and control over gateway uptime.","date":"2024-11-11"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"3Cloud solutions gateway performance monitoring Power BI PBIT template","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://3cloudsolutions.com/resources/monitoring-power-bi-on-premises-data-gateway-performance/","title":"Monitoring Power BI On-Premises Data Gateway Performance","domain":"3cloudsolutions.com","summary":"In June 2019, Microsoft made Gateway Monitoring much easier via the addition of structured logs that can be enabled in the configuration file, and by providing a starter Gateway Performance Power BI report template to visualize the results.\n...\nIn June 2019, Microsoft released a report template, which is a great place to start to get data loaded and processed.\nThere have been some great improvements to the template over the past several months, but in its current state, organizations would still have a lot of work to do to add more insightful, actionable visualizations, and a more intuitive report layout.\n**The Gateway Performance Report template,** developed by BlueGranite, uses Microsoft’s as a starting point but expands and builds upon it to provide an actionable, easy-to-navigate, and use, report.\nThe most notable features include:\n- A defined Gateway Node dimension, and classification of Gateway Objects by Cluster and Environment, such as Test and Production.\n- An optional toggle switch between default UTC and desired local time zone.\n- Parameters built specifically to track down long-running DirectQuery queries (threshold is in seconds) and long-running refreshes in Import mode (in minutes).\n...\n- The ability to run a comparison to a user-defined time period (also normalized) so that last week’s performance can be compared to last month, last quarter, etc.\n- Actionable, easy-to-understand visuals.\n- A full-fledged Data Dictionary to help user experience.\n...\nInstructions on how to set up Gateway Logging and the Microsoft Gateway Performance Power BI template download are available here.","date":"2020-02-11"},{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance","title":"Monitor and optimize on-premises data gateway performance","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"To monitor performance, gateway admins have traditionally depended on manually monitoring performance counters through the Windows Performance Monitor tool.\nWe now offer additional query logging and a Gateway Performance PBI template file to visualize the results.\nThis feature provides new insights into gateway usage.\nYou can use it to troubleshoot slow-performing queries.\nNote\nThis feature is currently available only for the on-premises data gateway in the standard mode.\nIt's not available for the personal mode.\n...\nThis feature is now turned on by default.\n...\nThere are other values in the config file `C:\\Program Files\\On-premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config` that you can update as needed:\n- **ReportFilePath**: Determines the path where the four log files are stored.\nBy default, this path is either *\\Users\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report* or *\\Windows\\ServiceProfiles\\PBIEgwService\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Report*.\nThe path depends on the OS version.\nIf you use a service account for the gateway other than *PBIEgwService*, replace this part of the path with the service account name.\n- **ReportFileCount**: Determines the number of log files of each kind to retain.\nThe default value is 10.\n- **ReportFileSizeInBytes**: Determines the size of the file to maintain.\nThe default value is 104,857,600.\n- **QueryExecutionAggregationTimeInMinutes**: Determines the number of minutes for which the query execution information is aggregated.\nThe default value is 5.\n- **SystemCounterAggregationTimeInMinutes**: Determines the number of minutes for which the system counter is aggregated.\nThe default value is 5.\nAfter you make the changes to the config file, restart the gateway for these config values to take effect.\nThe report files are now being generated in the location that you specified for **ReportFilePath**.\n...\nWhen you turn on this feature, four new log files are created:\n- The Query Execution Report\n- The Query Start Report\n- The Query Execution Aggregation Report\n- The System Counter Aggregation Report\n...\n### Visualize gateway performance\nNow, you can visualize the data that's in the log files.\n1. Download the Gateway Performance PBI template, and open it by using Power BI Desktop.\n2. In the dialog box that opens, check that the folder path matches the value in **ReportFilePath**.\n3. Select **Load**, and the template file starts loading the data from your log files.\nAll visuals are populated by using the data in the reports.\n4. Optionally, save this file as a PBIX, and publish it to your service for automatic refreshes.\nTo learn more, go to Publish semantic models and reports from Power BI Desktop.\n...\n1. In the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes** setting to **True**, and then save.\n```\n<setting name=\"StreamBeforeRequestCompletes\" serializeAs=\"String\">\n<value>True</value>\n</setting>\n```\n2. In **On-premises data gateway** > **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2025-06-12"},{"url":"https://asankap.wordpress.com/2019/09/02/how-to-monitor-power-bi-gateway-using-a-power-bi-report/","title":"How to monitor Power BI Gateway using a Power BI report?","domain":"asankap.wordpress.com","summary":"In 2019-May release of Power BI on-premises gateway, it has introduced a new feature called Gateway Performance Monitoring.\nPower BI team has given this feature so that now you can monitor query activities, errors and resource usage of a gateway sever in a power report very easily.\n...\nPlease note that this feature is still in preview (as at now) and by default it is disabled.\nIf you are to enable it, you need to do some changes in a configuration file.\nFirstly, you need to find the “Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config” file inside your installation folder.\nUnless you have specified else, default location is “C:\\Program Files\\On-premises data gateway”.\nCheck Image:01\n...\nOpen this config file and you will notice that by default following settings are set to False.\nSet it as True and check for the “ReportFilePath’ setting to make sure where will the report data be saved once we enable this option.\nCheck Image:02/Image:03\n*Image:02: Settings to set as True*\n...\nAfter you have done the changes, save the configuration file and open the gateway application.\nIn-order to changes to affect you need to restart to gateway and you can do that by clicking restart now button in “Service Settings”.\nWhile doing so identify the user which has used to run Gateway Service inside your server.\nIn most cases it will be “NT SERVICE\\PBIEgwService” unless you have specified someone else.\n...\nOnce the gateway is restarted you can go and check the folder you set as report folder previously and you will notice that there are set of files inside it.\nOne thing to you must know that these files are inside service users “AppData” folder; Not your “AppData” folder.\n...\nIf you open these files you can notice that these are additional log files created by Gateway application.\nTo analyse these log data, download “Gateway Performance PBI template file” from following link.\nTemplate Download Link:\nhttp://download.microsoft.com/download/D/A/1/DA1FDDB8-6DA8-4F50-B4D0-18019591E182/GatewayPerformanceMonitoring.pbit\nDouble click on the template file and it will open Power BI desktop application and ask for your report data folder path.\nBy default, it will automatically pick correct path and if not, you can change path and click load to view the report.\nCheck Image: 06\nIt will open a report which contains 5 report pages.\nI have listed what kind of information available in each page in below table.\n|||\n|--|--|\n|Query Execution By Data Source|Query Execution information by data source.\nIncluding Query Execution time, Data Processing time and more.|\n|Query Execution By Query Type|Query Execution information by query type.\nQuery type can either be refresh query or direct query.|\n|System Counters Over Time|System counter such as CPU and Memory usage of gateway.|\n|Average Execution And Errors|Average execution time, number of successful/failed queries, No of failures by query type.|\n|Data Error|All the Data errors occurred when running queries.|","date":"2019-09-02"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v7G8mwBVQJg","title":"On-Premises Data Gateway Monitoring Strategy. Why should I care?","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"Therefore, after publishing the report on Power BI Service, you will certainly take care to install and configure an On-premises Data Gateway in Enterprise mode on a machine from which the data source is reachable, so that you can schedule a dataset refresh appropriately.\n...\n{ts:1190} gives you a detailed information about the system resources that you have in a specific amount of time so you will set up the amount of time doing the aggregation and you will see which is the uh the results uh like cpu ram\n...\n…\n{ts:1587} logging system and to be honest they did something uh if you go to this uh link with the qr code system you will uh you will land in this page where you can find a pbit uh\n{ts:1606} power power bi template file that you can simply download and point to the your custom location so you can simply extract the logs in a in a folder using maybe the export logs\n{ts:1623} from the um from the on-premises app and you can point to them using this template the pros of this solution is that it's obviously free because microsoft supports this on their docs\n{ts:1642} and gives you the possibility to download without any fee it's very quick to install because you download it and you open without any problem it's a power bi template thank you very much uh olivia for putting the link in the chat\n{ts:1660} and then uh it's uh obviously uh 100 compatible with all the power bi gateways so you can download this power bi template and use it on all your customer because it's 100 compatible it uses only the logs that you can extract from any\n{ts:1681} gateway it by default it allows to get some insights from the logging because it picks up the log files and then gives you some report out of the box some data had already been prepared in\n{ts:1698} some way you have some uh some measures some data application or something like that some preparation is already there and probably you can give insights to power bi teams without the need of\n...\n{ts:2143} do is to create a better look and feel for for sure because the the actual is is not use of usable at all um we can add other tables other dimensions that are not already in the standard power bi\n{ts:2159} template like extracting the data sources that are used during our refresh so in this way creating a dimension like data sources you will be able to filter in your reports just the um useful data sources that you want to","date":"2022-02-09"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vGbfDScNU6A","title":"PBIPT #43 - Power BI Gateway Monitoring","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"With each cluster having multiple servers that are responsible for connecting their data sources to the Power BI service in the cloud, investigating errors or performance issues across the gateway logs can become a complex task.\nThis presentation aims to show a solution to help Power BI admins to:\n• Quickly access all gateway logs\n• Explore the gateway logs, counters & queries on a pre-built Power BI Report\nPower BI Gateway Monitor solution:\nhttps://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\n…","date":"2022-09-28"},{"url":"https://github.com/kerski/pbi-dataops-monitoring","title":"kerski/pbi-dataops-monitoring: Templates for monitoring Power BI ...","domain":"github.com","summary":"Templates for monitoring Power BI components and related Power Platform elements for your data analytics projects.\n...\nEmbracing the DataOps principle \"Monitor Quality and Performance\", this repository provides a set of templates and instructions for monitoring your Power BI solution/projects.\n...\nTo help automate checks of the issues, the templates use two open-source customer connectors.\n...\nPower Query Custom Data Connector for Power BI REST APIs\n...\nPower Query Custom Data Connector for Power BI REST APIs\nPower Query Custom Data Connector for Power Automate REST APIs\n#### On-Premises Gateway\nThe custom data connectors will need to be installed in the a Power BI Gateway in order to refresh the monitoring template in the Power BI Service.\nFor more information on installing a custom data connector with a gateway please see: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/connect-data/service-gateway-custom-connectors.\n## Installation\n1. Download the appropriate .pbit file from the latest Release section.\n2. Open the .pbit template.\n3. You will see a prompt as shown in the Figure 1\n*Figure 1 - Example of Template Prompt*\n1. In the field \"Workspace(s) Query\" enter the OData query that identifies the workspace or workspaces you wish to monitor.\nFor example if the workspace you wish to monitor is named 'Test1' you will enter \"name eq 'Test1'\".\nFor another example if the workspaces you wish to monitor are named 'Test1' and 'Test2' you will enter \"name eq 'Test1' or name eq 'Test2'\".\n1. Select the \"Load\" button.\nIf this is your first time using the custom data connectors you will be prompted to sign into Office 365.\nPlease follow the instructions to sign in and then choose the \"Connect\" button.\n*Figure 2 - Sign In Prompt*\n1. and the template will load.\n*Figure 3 - Example of Refresh In Progress* 6.\nWhen completed you should see the Home tab of the monitoring report.\n*Figure 4 - Example of Power BI Monitoring Report*\n1. Please save this file.\nIf you do not have a gateway installed, you will need to refresh this file locally to pull the latest information.\n...\nSince the connector uses the OAuth of the logged in accountto get the Power Automate flows, it's possible that you do not want to monitor all the Power Automate flows that are retrieved by the template.\nTo specify the Power Automate flows you wish to monitor, within the template follow these steps:\n1. Open Power Query.\n2. Navigate to the table \"Power Automate Flows\".\n3. Filter the \"Name\" and specify with flows you wish to monitor.\n*Figure 5 - Screenshot of where to filter the Power Automate flows that you wish to monitor.*\n## Exceptions with unscheduled Power BI Datasets\nIf there are datasets that do not require an automated refresh scheduled with the Power BI Dataset settings, you can add those datasets to an exception list.\nWithin the template, please follow these steps:\n1. Open Power Query.\n2. Navigate to the table \"Refresh Exception List\"\n3. Within the \"Query Settings\" pane, click the cog icon next to the \"Source\" step.\n*Figure 6 - Screenshot of where to add/update datasets on the refresh exception list.*\n1. Add/Update the names of the datasets you wish to have an exception.\nWhen complete, click the \"OK\" button.\nPlease note the name of the dataset must match **exactly** the name in the Power BI service.","date":"2023-03-24"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"SQLvariant gateway PowerShell governance gist","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://gist.github.com/SQLvariant?direction=desc&sort=updated","title":"SQLvariant's gists · GitHub","domain":"gist.github.com","summary":"SQLvariant / **GatewayClusters.ps1**\nLast active\nDecember 9, 2024 20:44\nGet the Governance Data You Need Out of Your Power BI Gateways with PowerShell\nThis file contains bidirectional Unicode text that may be interpreted or compiled differently than what appears below.\n...\nLearn more about bidirectional Unicode characters\n| |<# Make sure you have the modules installed.|\n|--|--|\n| |You only need the ImportExcel module if you want to export the results to Excel files.\n#>|\n| |Install-Module DataGateway|\n| |Install-Module ImportExcel|\n| | |\n| |<# Discovery #>|\n| |# All Gateway Clusters in your tenant.|\n| |Get-DataGatewayCluster -Scope Organization|\n| | |\n| |# All nodes of all Gateway Clusters in your tenant.|\n...\nPowerShell Notebook showing how to use both cmdlets available for extracting Power BI audit log events\nLoading","date":"2021-08-03"},{"url":"https://gist.github.com/SQLvariant/32432b6dc6e72ecc88ac58360733733f?short_path=f50de6a","title":"Export details of your PowerPlatform Gateway assets from your entire tenant.","domain":"gist.github.com","summary":"- - Save SQLvariant/32432b6dc6e72ecc88ac58360733733f to your computer and use it in GitHub Desktop.\n...\nExport details of your PowerPlatform Gateway assets from your entire tenant.\n...\n| |\"Get-DataGatewayCluster -Scope Organization |\\r\\n\",|","date":"2019-12-10"},{"url":"https://www.sqlservercentral.com/blogs/get-the-governance-data-you-need-out-of-your-power-bi-gateways-with-powershell","title":"Get the Governance Data You Need Out of Your Power BI Gateways ...","domain":"www.sqlservercentral.com","summary":"The Power Platform team has released a preview of their PowerShell module for managing your Data Gateways.\nYou can install it now from the PowerShell Gallery using PowerShell Core.\n...\nThe DataGateway module comes with 23 cmdlets.\nSo far, the ones I find myself using the most are Get-DataGatewayCluster, Get-DataGatewayClusterDataSource, & Get-DataGatewayClusterDataSourceUser cmdlets.\n(Those last two are the ones I did a very small amount of work on to make more user friendly.)\nYou can pipe them all together and get the data source & permissions data out of your On-Premises Data Gateway connections.\nThis makes it very simple to gather the data you need for an audit, or other governance needs.\nOther important questions these cmdlets can answer are things like:\n- How many On-Premises Data Gateways do I even have in my organization?\n- How many of those are Enterprise vs. Personal Gateway.\n- How many member-servers do I have in a Cluster?\n- What version of the Data Gateway software are these machines running?\nI have included a Gist with some of the data I’m collecting out of the\nOn-Premises Data Gateways with PowerShell.\nI hope you find them useful","date":"2019-10-15"},{"url":"https://gist.github.com/SQLvariant/8c06509c13c6b311ac67164b685b260c","title":"Common SQL PowerShell examples for the SqlServer module","domain":"gist.github.com","date":"2019-03-14"},{"url":"https://github.com/itsnotaboutthecell/PowerBI-cmdlets/blob/master/notebooks/PowerBI_Gateway_Overview.ipynb","title":"PowerBI-cmdlets/notebooks/PowerBI_Gateway_Overview.ipynb at master · itsnotaboutthecell/PowerBI-cmdlets","domain":"github.com","date":"2020-06-27"},{"url":"https://gist.github.com/SQLvariant/e15050c500a24cca07cd76935a98783a","title":"SQL PowerShell Demos for Spring of 2017","domain":"gist.github.com","summary":"| |Install-Module SqlServer|\n...\n| |<# If you don't have admin #>|\n| |Install-Module SqlServer -Scope CurrentUser|\n| | |\n| |<# Find the commands #>|\n| |Get-Command -Module SqlServer -CommandType Cmdlet ||\n| |Out-GridView|\n...\n| |<# What if you need to install on a server behind a firewall #>|\n| |Save-Module SqlServer -Path c:\\temp\\|\n| | |\n| |<# Just in case you run into an issue with the old SQLPS module #>|\n...\n| |Invoke-Item (Get-Item SQLSERVER:\\SQL\\LOCALHOST\\DEFAULT).BackupDirectory|\n...\n| |Get-SqlDatabase -ServerInstance localhost ||\n| |Where { $_.Name -ne 'tempdb' } ||\n| |Backup-SqlDatabase -CompressionOption On;|\n...\n| |You probably need to change this to something like 'http://MyReportServer/ReportServer'.\nIf you have a named SSRS instance 'http://MyReportServer/ReportServer_SQL2016' #>|\n| |$SSRSInstance = 'http://localhost/ReportServer_SQL2016'|\n...\n| |<# Loop through Registered Servers & deploy the Setup.SQL file to each instance|\n| |You can also use a Central Management Server to list your SQL servers by swapping 'Database Engine Server Group' for 'Central Management Server Group' #>|\n| |#foreach ($RegisteredSQLs IN dir -recurse SQLSERVER:\\SQLRegistration\\'Database Engine Server Group'\\Host\\ | where {$_.Mode -ne 'd'} )|\n| |#{|\n| |#Invoke-Sqlcmd -ServerInstance $RegisteredSQLs.Name -Database msdb -InputFile $SQLFile|","date":"2017-05-22"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"devladlog on-premises data gateway automated health check script","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://www.devladlog.com/articles/power-bi/2025-11-17-on-premises-data-gateway-configuration-management/","title":"On-Premises Data Gateway: Configuration and Management","domain":"www.devladlog.com","summary":"### Automated Health Check Script\n```\n## Gateway health monitoring script (run via scheduled task every 5 minutes)\nfunction Test-GatewayHealth {\n```powershell\nparam(\n[string]$EmailTo = \"ops@contoso.com\",\n[string]$SmtpServer = \"smtp.contoso.com\"\n$report = @{\nTimestamp = Get-Date\nServerName = $env:COMPUTERNAME\nChecks = @()\nOverallStatus = \"Healthy\"\n## Check 1: Gateway service running\n$service = Get-Service -Name \"PBIEgwService\" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue\n$serviceCheck = @{\nName = \"Gateway Service\"\nStatus = if ($service -and $service.Status -eq \"Running\") { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"❌ Fail\" }\nDetails = \"Status: $($service.Status)\"\n$report.Checks += $serviceCheck\nif ($serviceCheck.Status -like \"*Fail*\") {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Unhealthy\"\n## Check 2: CPU usage\n$cpu = (Get-Counter \"\\Processor(_Total)\\% Processor Time\").CounterSamples[0].CookedValue\n$cpuCheck = @{\nName = \"CPU Usage\"\nStatus = if ($cpu -lt 80) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$([math]::Round($cpu, 1))%\"\n$report.Checks += $cpuCheck\nif ($cpu -gt 90) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n## Check 3: Available memory\n$memAvailMB = (Get-Counter \"\\Memory\\Available MBytes\").CounterSamples[0].CookedValue\n$memCheck = @{\nName = \"Available Memory\"\nStatus = if ($memAvailMB -gt 2048) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$([math]::Round($memAvailMB, 0)) MB\"\n$report.Checks += $memCheck\nif ($memAvailMB -lt 1024) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n## Check 4: Gateway log errors (last hour)\n$logPath = \"C:\\Users\\*\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\On-premises data gateway\\Gateway*.log\"\n$logs = Get-ChildItem $logPath | Sort-Object LastWriteTime -Descending | Select-Object -First 1\nif ($logs) {\n$recentErrors = Get-Content $logs.FullName -Tail 1000 |\nWhere-Object { $_ -match \"ERROR|FATAL\" } |\nWhere-Object { (Get-Date) - [datetime]::Parse(($_ -split '\\|')[0]) -lt (New-TimeSpan -Hours 1) }\n$errorCheck = @{\nName = \"Recent Errors\"\nStatus = if ($recentErrors.Count -eq 0) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$($recentErrors.Count) errors in last hour\"\n}\n$report.Checks += $errorCheck\nif ($recentErrors.Count -gt 10) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n}\n## Check 5: Disk space\n$disk = Get-WmiObject Win32_LogicalDisk -Filter \"DeviceID='C:'\"\n$freeSpaceGB = [math]::Round($disk.FreeSpace / 1GB, 2)\n$diskCheck = @{\nName = \"Disk Space (C:)\"\nStatus = if ($freeSpaceGB -gt 10) { \"✅ Pass\" } else { \"⚠️ Warning\" }\nDetails = \"$freeSpaceGB GB free\"\n$report.Checks += $diskCheck\nif ($freeSpaceGB -lt 5) {\n$report.OverallStatus = \"Warning\"\n## Generate report\n$emailBody = @\"```\n<html>\n<head><style>\ntable { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }\n...\n.warning { color: orange; }\n.fail { color: red; }\n</style></head>\n<body>\n<h2>Gateway Health Report - $($report.ServerName)</h2>\n<p><strong>Timestamp:</strong> $($report.Timestamp)</p>\n<p><strong>Overall Status:</strong> <span class=\"$($report.OverallStatus.ToLower())\">$($report.OverallStatus)</span></p>\n...\nforeach ($check in $report.Checks) {\n$emailBody += \"<tr><td>$($check.Name)</td><td>$($check.Status)</td><td>$($check.Details)</td></tr>\"\n...\n</table>\n</body>\n</html>\n\"@\n```text\n## Send email if unhealthy or warning\nif ($report.OverallStatus -ne \"Healthy\") {\n$subject = \"⚠️ Gateway Health Alert - $($report.ServerName) - $($report.OverallStatus)\"\nSend-MailMessage -To $EmailTo -From \"gateway-monitor@contoso.com\" -Subject $subject `\n-Body $emailBody -BodyAsHtml -SmtpServer $SmtpServer\nWrite-Host \"Alert sent: $subject\" -ForegroundColor Yellow\n} else {\nWrite-Host \"✅ Gateway health check passed\" -ForegroundColor Green\nreturn $report```\n## Run health check\nTest-GatewayHealth\n```\n...\n## Analyze gateway logs for patterns and issues\nfunction Analyze-GatewayLogs {\n...\nfunction Update-Gateway {\n```powershell\n...\nWrite-Host \"Checking for gateway updates...\"\n-ForegroundColor Cyan\n## Get current gateway version\n$gatewayExe = \"C:\\Program Files\\On-premises data gateway\\EnterpriseGatewayConfigurator.exe\"\nif (-not (Test-Path $gatewayExe)) {\nWrite-Host \"❌ Gateway not found\" -ForegroundColor Red\nreturn\n$currentVersion = (Get-Item $gatewayExe).VersionInfo.FileVersion\nWrite-Host \"Current version: $currentVersion\" -ForegroundColor White\n## Download latest installer\n$downloadUrl = \"https://download.microsoft.com/download/D/A/1/DA1FDDB8-6DA8-4F50-B4D0-18019591E182/GatewayInstall.exe\"\n$installerPath = \"$env:TEMP\\GatewayInstall_$(Get-Date -Format 'yyyyMMdd').exe\"\nWrite-Host \"Downloading latest installer...\"\n-ForegroundColor Cyan\nInvoke-WebRequest -Uri $downloadUrl -OutFile $installerPath\n$newVersion = (Get-Item $installerPath).VersionInfo.FileVersion\nWrite-Host \"Latest version: $newVersion\" -ForegroundColor White\nif ($currentVersion -eq $newVersion) {\nWrite-Host \"✅ Gateway is up to date\" -ForegroundColor Green\nreturn\nWrite-Host \"Update available: $currentVersion → $newVersion\" -ForegroundColor Yellow\n...\nStart-Process -FilePath $installerPath -ArgumentList \"-quiet\", \"-norestart\", \"-log\", $logPath -Wait -NoNewWindow\nWrite-Host \"✅ Gateway updated successfully\" -ForegroundColor Green\nWrite-Host \"Log: $logPath\"\n...\n## Run update (with auto-restart)\nUpdate-Gateway -AutoRestart\n...\n4. **Implement automated monitoring** with health check scripts","date":"2025-11-17"},{"url":"https://www.devladlog.com/articles/power-bi/2025-07-13-data-gateway-operations-security-and-optimization-playbook-2025/","title":"On-Premises Data Gateway: Operations, Security, and Optimization Playbook (2025)","domain":"www.devladlog.com","summary":"```\n# Security monitoring script for On-Premises Data Gateway\n# Run daily as scheduled task\n$alerts = @()\n# Check 1: Service principal credentials expiring within 30 days\n$apps = Get-MgApplication -All\nforeach ($app in $apps) {\nforeach ($cred in $app.PasswordCredentials) {\n$daysUntilExpiry = ($cred.EndDateTime - (Get-Date)).Days\nif ($daysUntilExpiry -lt 30 -and $daysUntilExpiry -gt 0) {\n$alerts += @{\nType = \"CredentialExpiry\"\nSeverity = if ($daysUntilExpiry -lt 7) { \"Critical\" } else { \"Warning\" }\nMessage = \"$($app.DisplayName) credential expires in $daysUntilExpiry days\"\nAction = \"Rotate credential immediately\"\n}\n}\n}\n}\n# Check 2: Failed sign-in attempts (brute force detection)\n$failedSignIns = Get-MgAuditLogSignIn -Filter \"status/errorCode ne 0\" -Top 100\n$suspiciousIPs = $failedSignIns |\nGroup-Object -Property IpAddress |\nWhere-Object { $_.Count -gt 10 }\nforeach ($ip in $suspiciousIPs) {\n$alerts += @{\nType = \"BruteForce\"\nSeverity = \"Critical\"\nMessage = \"$($ip.Count) failed attempts from $($ip.Name)\"\nAction = \"Block IP in Conditional Access; investigate\"\n}\n}\n# Report\nif ($alerts.Count -gt 0) {\nWrite-Host \"=== SECURITY ALERTS ===\" -ForegroundColor Red\n$alerts | ForEach-Object {\nWrite-Host \"[$($_.Severity)] $($_.Message)\" -ForegroundColor $(\nif ($_.Severity -eq \"Critical\") { \"Red\" } else { \"Yellow\" }\n)\n}\n} else {\nWrite-Host \"No security alerts\" -ForegroundColor Green\n}\n```\n...\n|Availability|99.9%|< 99.5%|Synthetic monitoring|\n|API Response Time (P95)|< 2 seconds|> 5 seconds|Application Insights|\n|Error Rate|< 1%|> 5%|Log Analytics|\n|Active Users (DAU)|Trending up|Drop > 20% week-over-week|Usage analytics|\n|Data Sync Latency|< 5 minutes|> 15 minutes|Custom metric|\n|Storage Utilization|< 80%|> 90%|Platform metrics|\n### Alert Configuration\n```\n{\n\"alertRules\": [\n{\n\"name\": \"High Error Rate\",\n\"condition\": \"errorRate > 5% for 5 minutes\",\n\"severity\": \"Critical\",\n\"action\": [\"email-oncall\", \"teams-channel\", \"pagerduty\"],\n\"runbook\": \"https://wiki.internal/runbooks/high-error-rate\"\n},\n{\n\"name\": \"Slow Response Time\",\n\"condition\": \"p95Latency > 5s for 10 minutes\",\n\"severity\": \"Warning\",\n\"action\": [\"teams-channel\"],\n\"runbook\": \"https://wiki.internal/runbooks/slow-response\"\n},\n{\n\"name\": \"Capacity Warning\",\n\"condition\": \"storageUsed > 80%\",\n\"severity\": \"Warning\",\n\"action\": [\"email-admin\"],\n\"runbook\": \"https://wiki.internal/runbooks/capacity-planning\"\n}\n]\n}\n...\n# Performance analysis script\nWrite-Host \"=== Performance Analysis for On-Premises Data Gateway ===\" -ForegroundColor Cyan\n# 1.\nCheck API call patterns\nWrite-Host \"Analyzing API call patterns...\"\n$apiMetrics = @{\nTotalCalls = 15432\n...\nErrorRate = \"0.8%\"\nThrottledCalls = 23\n}\n...\n# 2.\nIdentify optimization opportunities\n$optimizations = @(\n@{ Area = \"Caching\"; Impact = \"High\"; Effort = \"Low\";\n...\n- Monitor the 6 key metrics continuously; configure alerts with clear runbook links\n- Performance optimization is ongoing — run the analysis script monthly","date":"2025-07-13"},{"url":"https://www.devladlog.com/articles/power-bi/2025-07-10-data-gateway-developer-implementation-guide-2025/","title":"On-Premises Data Gateway: Developer Implementation Guide (2025)","domain":"www.devladlog.com","date":"2025-07-10"},{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-performance","title":"Monitor and optimize on-premises data gateway performance","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"1. Download the Gateway Performance PBI template, and open it by using Power BI Desktop.\n2. In the dialog box that opens, check that the folder path matches the value in **ReportFilePath**.\n3. Select **Load**, and the template file starts loading the data from your log files.\nAll visuals are populated by using the data in the reports.\n4. Optionally, save this file as a PBIX, and publish it to your service for automatic refreshes.\nTo learn more, go to Publish semantic models and reports from Power BI Desktop.\n...\n1. In the C:\\Program Files\\On-Premises data gateway\\Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll.config file, set the **StreamBeforeRequestCompletes** setting to **True**, and then save.\n...\n2. In **On-premises data gateway** > **Service Settings**, restart the gateway.","date":"2025-06-12"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=963KPiL2PZk","title":"055 - On Premises Data Gateway Monitoring Status - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"In this video, we are going to discuss how we can monitor the status of our On-premises Data Gateways through the use of Power BI Powershell CMDlets.\nWe will orchestrate these Powershell calls by using Power Automate Desktop.\n...\n{ts:125} that just to help save you some time if you're trying to set this up for yourself so number one\n{ts:129} you do need to download powershell 7 and powershell 7 is a prerequisite because\n{ts:136} of the power bi powershell command lines that we're going to use those have been written in powershell 7\n{ts:144} and as a result we need to make sure this is installed on our machine so here's where you can go ahead and\n{ts:150} grab the latest version of powershell and i will include a link in the description of this video\n{ts:157} to help you out from that perspective the other thing we're going to need is an azure ad\n{ts:165} app so when we go ahead and we run this process we are going to use service principle\n{ts:171} and we will need to go create that azure ad app in order to support\n{ts:175} that service principle requirement now when you go ahead and set this up the most important thing to do here\n{ts:181} is you've got api permissions and you need the power bi service and then these are the two operations\n{ts:188} that we're going to be calling underneath the hood tenant.read.all and tenantreadwrite.all\n{ts:195} now do note these do require admin consent and for good reason when we run this we are going to run\n{ts:201} this using an organization scope so we are going to go ahead and discover\n{ts:206} all gateways in our environment that is certainly a requirement for all of this monitoring scenario\n...\n{ts:224} this data gateway module now this is provided by microsoft and so\n{ts:229} that is what we need to do is we need to go ahead and install it because shortly here we're going to go\n{ts:234} ahead and import that as a part of our script and naturally it needs to be there in\n{ts:239} order for us to go ahead and use it once again more information at the following link i'll include that in the\n...\n{ts:268} inside of powershell now we're going to talk about two specifically the get data gateway\n{ts:274} cluster and the get data gateway cluster status and so let's talk a little bit about\n...\n{ts:311} can essentially discover all of the gateways that exist in our environment\n{ts:316} by using the get data gateway cluster command now this won't give us the status we have to use the\n{ts:324} id value that is returned from this call in order to actually get the status of a specific\n...\n{ts:350} and then here is the subsequent call that i just spoke about so get data gateway cluster\n{ts:355} status and as part of that we do need to provide that scope which in this case is\n{ts:359} that organization you can also have scope for you know an individual user\n{ts:364} as well but as i mentioned we want to focus across the enterprise and then what we do need to do is feed\n{ts:369} in a gateway cluster id which was the same id that we talked about in the previous\n{ts:376} slide itself now when we make this call we will see a status in this case it'll be none which essentially means offline\n...\n{ts:599} uh so i'll try to go sort of as reasonably slow as i can but the first thing we want to do is we\n{ts:605} want to make sure we get our excel spreadsheet set up so i would envision that this type of\n{ts:609} script gets run say every hour or maybe every half day or maybe once a day and so as a result we're going to\n{ts:615} have that excel file and we want to make sure we always have a fresh excel file\n{ts:620} that we can go ahead and leverage and so what i'm going to go ahead and do is i have a subflow here\n{ts:625} and the subflow is essentially going to copy a templated version of that spreadsheet and place it into a\n{ts:631} temp folder and then we're going to exit this subflow so if we take a look at what\n...\n{ts:646} into this working folder that we have here so that's step number one\n{ts:652} the next thing we're going to do is we're going to go ahead and run our powershell script\n{ts:656} now this is one of the scripts that's available on my blog post and if we look at it we'll find it here\n{ts:663} it's going to be getgatewaylist.ps1 let's go ahead and edit it and now as part of this what we're going to do is\n{ts:671} pass in argument so basically our app id our secret and our tenant we're then going to construct a secret\n{ts:676} string because we naturally need to do that and we will go ahead and we will\n{ts:682} connect to the number one we'll connect we'll create a connection to the gateway service account using\n{ts:689} those values then what we'll do is we'll import the data gateway\n{ts:694} module and then we will go ahead and execute the get data gateway cluster operation and we're going to do this at\n{ts:701} the organization scope and we're going to output the all of that data to a file itself\n...\n{ts:1013} split it again and we'll once again use a delimiter and then what we'll do is we will write\n{ts:1019} the value of the cluster name also into that excel spreadsheet uh sorry the cluster status into that\n{ts:1026} excel spreadsheet in this case it's column c now the last step in our loop um is we're then going to go ahead and\n{ts:1033} get the gateway name and we will go ahead and capture that from like the original uh text because","date":"2020-10-25"},{"url":"https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/data-integration/blob/master/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-log-files.md","title":"Configure log files for the on-premises data gateway - GitHub","domain":"github.com","summary":"1. Open the gateway configuration file Microsoft.PowerBI.EnterpriseGateway.exe.config, which by default should be located under \\Program Files\\On-premises data gateway.\n2. Before proceeding further, make a copy of this file just in case you need to restore it later.\n...\nApplicationFileTraceListener","date":"2019-07-16"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Power BI gateway monitoring Nagios Zabbix SCOM integration","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://www.zabbix.com/integrations/powerbi","title":"Power BI monitoring and integration with Zabbix","domain":"www.zabbix.com"},{"url":"https://www.zabbix.com/integrations","title":"Zabbix Integrations and Templates","domain":"www.zabbix.com","summary":"- ##### Power BI\n- ##### POWERCOM"},{"url":"https://www.zabbix.com/integrations/scom","title":"SCOM monitoring and integration with ...","domain":"www.zabbix.com","summary":"|Zabbix alerts in SCOM consoleOverall solution is build on top of SC Orchestrator, which is sitting in the middle between Zabbix monitoring server and SCOM server.wininframaster.blogspot.com/2017/01/zabbix-alerts-in-scom-console.html| | | | | |","date":"2017-01-01"},{"url":"https://www.zabbix.com/cz/integrations/nagios","title":"Nagios monitoring and integration with Zabbix","domain":"www.zabbix.com"},{"url":"http://wininframaster.blogspot.com/2017/01/zabbix-alerts-in-scom-console.html","title":"Zabbix alerts in SCOM console","domain":"wininframaster.blogspot.com","summary":"Overall solution is build on top of SC Orchestrator, which is sitting in the middle between Zabbix monitoring server and SCOM server.\nThe integration idea is:\n- Every alert raised in Zabbix is forwarded to the SCOM console for centralized viewing\n- If alert is closed in Zabbix, appropriate SCOM alert is closed as well\n- If somebody accidentally close Zabbix alert in SCOM console, synchronization will open again the same alert in SCOM\nSo lets go to the solution\nAt first we need to prepare Orchestrator.\nIt will have two interfaces:\n- For interactions with SCOM we are going to use System Center Integration Packs (in my case I used 2016 version).\nPlease note that SCOM integration pack does not require SCOM console installed on Orchestrator server any more.\nFrom this integration pack we will be using following activities:\n- Create Alert\n- Get Alert\n- Update Alert\n- For interactions with Zabbix there is no Out of the box integration component, so we will use PowerShell as universal solution :) Zabix has nice Web based API, which is using JSON-RPC 2.0 protocol and it is not very difficult to use PowerShell for talking to it.\n**Monitor Date/Time**activity is using to trigger my runbook every minute.\nIn Zabbix Alerts I'm calling PowerShell script to talk to Zabbix via JSON-RPC and grabbing all new and updated alerts.\nActivity\n**Get Alert Properties**is publishing all useful information about alert to the Orchestrator data bus.\nActivity\n**Get Existing Alert**is checking if such Zabbix alert already exists in SCOM console.\nIf not, I'm creating new one with separate runbook (for parallelism and speed purposes).\nThat separate runbook is very simple:\nTo be sure that Zabbix alert does not exists in SCOM, I'm flagging every SCOM alert with Zabbix alert id (putting it to the extended attribute 1 of SCOM alert).\nThe last two activities in main runbook are for updating existing SCOM alert.\nOne is for closing existing SCOM alert, after it is closed in Zabbix.\nThe second activity is for reopening SCOM alert.\nIt is useful if somebody accidentally closed SCOM alert but alert in Zabbix is still opened.\nJust to give you the idea how to communicate with Zabbix with PowerShell, this is part of my script\nThe result of this script is getting Token from Zabbix, which you are using in next communication wit Zabbix.\nExample of token:\nAfter this simple synchronization solution, you will be able to see in your SCOM console also Zabbix alerts as well.\n...\nFor correct working PowerShell activities in Orchestrator I used registry key HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Wow6432Node\\Microsoft\\.NETFramework with new DWORD entry and value of OnlyUseLatestCLR = 1.\nWith this setting my Run .NET activity is using latest available PowerShell on the host.\nFor PowerShell cmdlet Invoke-WebRequest you can have problem if you have not disabled First Run Wizard in IE.\nFor disabling I'm using this GPO setting: Computer Configuration - Policies - Administrative Templates - Windows Components - Internet Explorer.\nSet the policy Prevent running First Run Wizard to Enabled.","date":"2017-01-29"},{"url":"https://www.zabbix.com/ru/integrations/nagios","title":"Nagios monitoring and integration with Zabbix","domain":"www.zabbix.com"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"Fabric Monitor Carlo Consulting LLC gateway monitoring features","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://marketplace.microsoft.com/en-us/product/carloconsultingllc1611096483318.fabric_monitor_02?tab=overview","title":"Business Intelligence Monitoring Accelerator and Support","domain":"marketplace.microsoft.com","summary":"Introducing a comprehensive Data Monitoring solution designed specifically for technical leaders who manage Power BI and Microsoft Fabric environments.\nThis accelerator is engineered to pull data from Microsoft Power BI APIs, Microsoft Fabric APIs, and Microsoft Graph APIs, creating a holistic view of activities and a detailed catalog of items across your entire Power BI and Microsoft Fabric Tenant.\nKey Features:\nUser Usage Metrics: Gain insights into user activities and interactions within your Power BI and Fabric environments.\nThis feature helps organizations of all sizes adopt and manage Power BI effectively.\nGateway Monitoring: Monitor the health and performance of your data gateways to ensure seamless data flow and connectivity.\nSemantic Model Temperature Monitoring: Keep track of the performance and usage of your semantic models, ensuring they are optimized for your business needs.\nCapacity Usage Metrics Logging: Understand how your capacities are being utilized over time, identify bottlenecks, and optimize resource allocation.\nLong-term Data Storage: Store all monitoring data for as long as needed, providing historical insights and trend analysis.\nDaily Admin Settings Logging: Each data store all admin settings for a comprehensive audit trail for any property or setting change within the Power BI administration portal.\n...\nWe also provide technical support for the Fabric Unified Admin Monitoring (FUAM) project, an open-source initiative that provides extensive monitoring capabilities for Power BI and Fabric environments.\nFUAM is designed with a modular architecture, allowing for easy extension and customization to meet specific customer needs.\n...\nBeyond the standard features of FUAM, our custom solutions for Entra Graph API and Semantic Model Temperature monitoring can be added to the base code of FUAM.\nWhether you need more detailed analytics or specific monitoring modules, this support solution can be customized to fit your unique needs for your business.","date":"2026-05-24"},{"url":"https://jumpstart.fabric.microsoft.com/catalog/fpm-gateway-monitoring/","title":"Fabric Platform Monitoring - Gateway Monitoring","domain":"jumpstart.fabric.microsoft.com","summary":"This jumpstart deploys an end-to-end **On-Premises Data Gateway observability stack** into your Microsoft Fabric workspace — two Eventstreams that receive heartbeat and report telemetry from a PowerShell uploader running on the gateway host, an Eventhouse-backed\n```\nGateway Monitoring\n```\nKQL database that durably stores every event, and a DirectQuery Power BI semantic model + report with six built-in pages (Jobs, Job Details, Queries, Running Jobs, Gateways, System Counters) for live operational insight.\n...\nInstalls\n``` ms-fabric-cli ``` , looks up the two Eventstreams'\n``` CustomEndpoint ``` source connection strings via the Fabric CLI, and writes\n``` ./builtin/config.json ``` — the configuration file the gateway-side PowerShell uploader consumes.\nSix user-editable variables seed the config:\n``` HeartbeatEnable ``` ,\n``` HeartbeatInterval ``` ,\n``` ReportSendInterval ``` ,\n``` ReportRetention ``` ,\n``` TenatId ``` ,\n``` AppId ``` .|\n|``` GatewayMonitoringHeartbeat ```|Eventstream|``` CustomEndpoint ``` source named\n``` Heartbeat ``` . Single destination: the Eventhouse, writing to the\n``` GatewaysHeartbeat ``` table via DirectIngestion with mapping\n``` GatewaysHeartbeat_mapping ``` .|\n|``` GatewayMonitoringReports ```|Eventstream|``` CustomEndpoint ``` source named\n``` Reports ``` . Single destination: the Eventhouse, writing to the\n``` GatewayReports-Raw ``` table via DirectIngestion with mapping\n``` GatewayReports-Raw_mapping ``` .|\n|``` Fabric Platform Monitoring ```|Eventhouse|Container for the KQL database below.|\n|``` Gateway Monitoring ```|KQLDatabase|All telemetry lives here.\n``` GatewaysHeartbeat ``` and\n``` GatewayReports-Raw ``` are the ingest tables; downstream KQL functions and views (\n``` GatewayNodeStatusWithInfo ``` ,\n``` QueryExecutionUnified ``` ,\n``` QueryConnections ``` ,\n``` SystemCounters ``` ) are what the semantic model queries.|\n|``` Gateway Monitoring ```|SemanticModel|DirectQuery Power BI model over the KQL database.\nSix query-group parameters —\n``` KustoURL ``` ,\n``` KustoDB ``` ,\n``` DaysBack ``` ,\n``` DateToSearch ``` ,\n``` SelectedGateways ``` ,\n``` SelectedClusters ``` — drive four DirectQuery tables (\n``` Gateway Node Info ``` ,\n``` Query Execution ``` ,\n``` Query Connections ``` ,\n``` System Counters ``` ) plus import tables for\n``` Calendar ``` ,\n``` Period ``` ,\n``` Execution Measures ``` , and the counter measure parameter.\n``` Execution Measures ``` contains 18 measures for jobs, queries, durations, and disk I/O.|\n|``` Gateway Monitoring ```|Report|Power BI report bound to the semantic model.\nSix pages: Jobs, Job Details (drillthrough), Queries, Running Jobs, Gateways, System Counters.\nPage refresh is enabled on Jobs, Queries, and System Counters at 5 minutes, Running Jobs at 10 seconds, and Gateways at 30 seconds.|\n...\nThe\n```\nGateway Monitoring\n```\nsemantic model uses **DirectQuery** against the KQL DB over the\n```\nKustoURL\n```\nparameter.\nFour DirectQuery tables map to KQL views / functions:|Table|Source|What's in it|\n|--|--|--|\n|``` Gateway Node Info ```|View\n``` GatewayNodeStatusWithInfo ```|One row per gateway node — cluster, machine, OS, cores, memory, status, last-seen times.|\n|``` Query Execution ```|Function\n``` QueryExecutionUnified(DateToSearch, DaysBack, ...)\n```|Per-activity execution detail — start/end, duration, success, query text, spooling I/O, data source.|\n...\n|``` System Counters ```|Function\n...\n```|Per-counter min/avg/max aggregations — including the divide-by-cores CPU correction in\n...\n|``` Calendar ```|Static M-query|Standard date dimension for time-intelligence.|\n...\nThe **report** has six pages:\n- **Jobs**— landing page, auto-refresh every 5 minutes, slicer-driven day range.\n- **Job Details**— drillthrough target.\n...\n```\n...\n```\n.\n- **Queries**— per-query detail across the chosen window.\n...\nmeasure).\n...\n."},{"url":"https://github.com/ecotte/Fabric-Monitoring-RTI/blob/main/README.md","title":"Fabric-Monitoring-RTI/README.md at main · ecotte/Fabric-Monitoring-RTI","domain":"github.com","summary":"Platform administrators face the challenge of observing the activities within the entire platform.\nThere are multiple sources that provide information, gateway locks, audit logs, and the platform inventory itself.\nAdditionally, there is a need to obtain this data quickly to observe and react to events automatically.\nTo address this, a solution has been developed based on Fabric Real-Time Intelligence (RTI).\nThis solution extracts information from audit logs through the API, gateway locks via a script included in the solution, and the inventory of the whole platform through the API.\nThe following architecture illustrates how the solution interacts with different components to receive, process, store, present dashboards, and react to the platform's information.\nIt also enables longer retention of logs for historical purposes and allows comparison of the current state with previous states to create custom solutions.\nThis solution uses Microsoft Fabric to address these issues by providing:\n- In-App Configurations\n- Real-Time Dashboard\n- PowerShell Setup Configurations for the Gateway Events\nBenefits include faster incident response, improved health analytics, and streamlined operations, consequently enhancing overall efficiency and reducing downtime.\n...\nThe following Fabric items are deployed and used:\n- Eventstreams:\n- GatewayMonitoringHeartbeat\n- To receive the gateway heartbeat\n- GatewayMonitoringReports\n- To receoive the gateway reports\n- Eventhouse:\n- Platform and Audit DB\n- To process, store and query all the information ingested\n- Notebooks:\n- Monitoring Audit Logs\n- Extract the Audit logs from the API and ingest them incrementally in the Eventhouse.\n- Recommended to be configured to run every 5 min.\n- Monitoring Extraction Refreshables:\n- Extract the refreshables from all the capacities and stores the last refresh incrementally.\n- Recommended to be configured to run every 5 min.\n- Monitoring Extraction Scanner:\n- Extract the full inventory with the Scanner API.\n- Creates the snapshot and loads incrementally.\n- Recommended to be configured to run every 30 min.\n- Monitoring Extraction Inventory:\n- Extracts the following information at tenant level:\n- Capacities\n- Apps\n- Domains\n- Tenant Settings\n- Workspace Delegated Settings\n- Capacity Delegated Settings\n- Domain Delegated Settings\n- Gateways and Members\n- Gateway Connections\n- Git Connections\n- Recommended to be configured to run every 1 day.\n...\nCreate a workspace and import the Platform Monitoring Setup Notebook . Follow the instructions for the first run.","date":"2024-05-16"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=37UyNx5v8hI","title":"DP-700 Monitor Hub in Fabric","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"Monitoring data ingestion, transformation, and refresh processes is crucial to keep your analytics running smoothly.\nIn this video, we’ll explore the Microsoft Fabric Monitor Hub, the place for tracking data pipeline executions, model refreshes, and Spark jobs.\n...\n{ts:19} that information and how can you monitor in fabric well you can use the fabric monitor hop and what you can see in the\n{ts:27} monitor hop is the data pipeline execution history data flow executions data mtin semantic model refreshes and\n{ts:35} also spark chops and notebook execution history so all these executions you can see in your monitor Hub so let's go into\n{ts:43} Fabric and have a look into how that actually works and where you can find it here we are in Fabric in one of my\n{ts:49} workspaces if I want to go to the monitor Hub I just go to the left here and I see this symbol monitor if I click\n{ts:55} on this you see the monitor environment and what does it say if you interact the status of the activities across all the\n{ts:61} workspaces or all of them for which you have permission within Fabric and you can also filter like now we see all the\n{ts:69} items that I have access to in all the workspaces but we could pick a different location we could fil on the start time\n{ts:77} on the status these kind of things and we can also show more options like if I want to see more information like\n{ts:86} refresh type I just pick one and then go up to apply it it is added here so I see if it's scheduled or on the month so\n{ts:93} what do we see here we see like the activity name the status and we see that one has failed here item type start time\n{ts:102} who submitted it the location so that's the workspace and refresh type here and now if I click on for example the first\n{ts:110} one it succeeded and I click on it then I get the details when it was executed at the right side here I see the\n{ts:119} duration if it was scheduled if there's a gate there and if there was an error so going back to the hub I go to the one\n...\n{ts:155} dots you can also say retry and then it would try again to refresh here so this monitor Hub is like super handy because\n{ts:165} you see all the activities in all the workspaces that you have access to so you have everything collected there so\n{ts:173} let's make sure that you also have a look there","date":"2025-04-22"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0HVN53x87yg","title":"Microsoft Fabric Monitoring | DP-700 Exam Prep - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"🔍 What you will learn in this video:\n• How to use the Monitoring Hub to track pipeline runs, notebooks, and semantic model refreshes\n• How the Capacity Metrics App helps you monitor capacity unit usage and storage consumption\n• What Workspace Monitoring is and how to enable it using Eventstream and Eventhouse\n• Overview of the Admin Monitoring Workspace for tenant-wide governance and usage tracking\n• How to monitor Warehouse connections, sessions, and queries using DMVs\n• How to analyze historical query data using Query Insights views like exec_requests_history and frequently_run_queries\n...\nBasically, monitoring hub is a central built-in dashboard that tracks recent activities\n{ts:87} across entire Marx fabric tenant.\nSo from here you can see activities that are happening and have happened in all\n{ts:94} the workspaces you have access to.\nMonitoring hub will then help you to track runs, check for errors and\n{ts:101} understand what has recently happened.\nFrom this hub, you can easily filter, sort, and even drill down the runs and\n{ts:108} activities that you are interested in.\nAlso, a good thing to note is that monitoring hub view can be customized\n{ts:114} and you can add many new columns to the view that are not there by default, but could be useful to have.\n...\nBut what exactly is monitored here?\nIf we\n{ts:128} think about monitoring hub in the context of DP700 exam, most of the data engineering tools can be monitored here\n{ts:135} like data pipelines, data flows, genos, notebooks, park jobs and semantic model refreshes and also many other items that\n…\nHowever, the monitoring hub displays activities only for fabric items you\n...\n{ts:205} it with new features.\nSo with this app, you can monitor your capacity consumption and then make decisions like\n…\nSo what exactly is monitored here?\nThe most common thing is\n{ts:234} the capacity consumption all the way to the item level since here we can see how much capacity units each item is\n{ts:242} consuming which can be very helpful information to identify poorly built data pipelines or other solutions that\n{ts:248} are not very capacity optimized.\nThen from the same report we can get information about storage consumption\n{ts:256} since that will generate costs as well.\nSo we can see how much data we have.\nThen there is this relatively new\n{ts:263} feature that enables separating spark compute from the capacity to its own compute pool which means that it\n…\n...\nSo to enable workspace monitoring, you have to go to workspace settings and there you can\n{ts:339} find this monitoring tab and there you are able to create the monitoring event host for the workspace.\nThis will then\n{ts:346} create some new items to the workspace.\none event stream called monitoring event stream that collects data about the\n{ts:353} workspace and then monitoring event house and monitoring KQL database to that event house to which the event\n{ts:360} stream writes the workspace log data and there are many different tables for different types of log data that is\n...\nThe workspace\n{ts:463} itself contains few pre-made reports that can be used for monitoring.\nThe admin monitoring workspace is\n{ts:468} automatically available in fabric and it is installed the first time it is accessed.\nSo you don't really have to do\n...\nThen we can get information about workspace items like how many items there are in a workspace and who\n{ts:521} owns those items and how many users have access to those items.\n...\n{ts:528} provided here.\n...\nSome warehouse and SQL endpoint monitoring can be done via capacity metrics app that we already\n{ts:562} covered.\nBut there are other ways and methods that can be used for monitoring.\nLet's first talk about monitoring\n{ts:569} connections, sessions, and requests.\nmeaning the users and entities that are connected to warehouse and things they\n{ts:576} are currently doing there.\nFor monitoring those things, we can use dynamic management views or DMVs.\nThere\n{ts:583} are three different DMVs that are provided automatically for warehouse and SQL endpoint that you can use to get\n{ts:590} insights you need.\nThey are all found in sush schema that is created automatically with warehouse and SQL\n{ts:596} endpoint.\nFirst we have DMX connections that gives information about each connection that has been established.\nSo\n{ts:605} this view gets you connection information like IP addresses, is the connection encrypted and what is the\n{ts:611} connection protocol and so on.\nThen we have DMXX sessions that tells information about each session that has\n{ts:619} been authenticated.\nFrom this view, you get information like the login name of the user, status of the session, meaning\n{ts:626} that is it actively doing anything or just leaving, how much memory and CPU the session is using and so on.\nThen\n{ts:633} lastly, we have DMX requests that gives information about the active requests that the sessions are doing.\nFor\n{ts:641} example, what type of SQL queries are being run at this moment.\n...\nFor this information, we have a\n{ts:740} dedicated query inside schema that has four views that can provide information about past and active queries and also\n...\nThen lastly we have frequently run queries view.\n{ts:813} Like the name implies this view tells information about frequently run queries.\nThis has some duplicate\n{ts:820} information with the previous view but a bit more detailed statistics about the past executions like min and max\n{ts:827} execution times and how many times query failed etc.\n...\n{ts:888} Also, there is a way in the fabric UI to access query insight information.\nWhen using warehouse or SQL endpoint, there\n{ts:895} is this query activity button in the top bar.\nClicking that button will open this view where you are able to access the\n{ts:902} same information that those query activity views have.","date":"2025-07-27"},{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/hu-hu/fabric/admin/monitoring-hub","title":"A Monitor hub használata - Microsoft Fabric","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"A Microsoft Fabric *Monitor* központ lehetővé teszi, hogy a felhasználók központi helyről figyeljék a Microsoft Fabric-tevékenységeket.\nA figyelőközpontot bármely Fabric-felhasználó használhatja, a figyelőközpont azonban csak a megtekintésre jogosult Fabric-elemek tevékenységeit jeleníti meg.\nA figyelőközpont az alábbi Hálóelemek tevékenységeit jeleníti meg:\n- Adatfolyamatok\n- Adatfolyamok\n- Datamarts\n- Lakehouses\n- Jegyzetfüzetek\n- Szemantikai modellek\n- Spark-feladatdefiníciók\n...\nA monitorközpont megnyitásához az In Fabricben válassza **a Figyelés** lehetőséget a navigációs panelen.\nA figyelőközpont táblaűrlapon jeleníti meg az információkat.\nA hálótevékenységek a kezdési időpontjuknak megfelelően jelennek meg, a tábla tetején pedig a legújabb tevékenységek láthatók.\nMinden Hálóelem legfeljebb 100 tevékenységet jelenít meg.\nAz előzmények 30 napig maradnak meg, és megtekinthetők az *Előzménynézet* lehetőséggel.\n...\nA kulcsszó keresőmezője segítségével a tevékenység neve alapján kereshet adott tevékenységeket.\nA keresés a betöltött adatokon történik, nem az adatbázis összes tevékenységén.","date":"2024-05-11"}],"error":null},{"ok":true,"spec":{"query":"martinskeem powerbi-powershell gateway status event log","limit":6},"result":[{"url":"https://github.com/martinskeem/powerbi-powershell","title":"martinskeem/powerbi-powershell - GitHub","domain":"github.com","summary":"Eihter clone this repository or install from PowerShell Gallery using this code:\n```\nInstall-Module -Name powerbi-powershell\n...\n### Report gateway status to event log\nA typical production requirement might be to implement some sort of surveillance of the gateway.\nBelow sample will report current activity state of gateway (active, inactive) into the Application Event Log, which then, in turn could be monitored by a central function.\nBefore the first time the script is executed, the event log source must be created using:\n```\nNew-EventLog -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -LogName \"Application\"\n```\nOnce the source is created, below can be scheduled to run e.g. every 5 minutes after filling in the gateway name and details of the Azure AD app.\n```\n$gatewayName = \"\" # insert name of Gateway to check here.\n$clientId = \"\" # The ID of the Application ID of the App Registration in Azure AD\n$redirectUri = \"\" # One of the redirect URIs provided in the App Registration in Azure AD\n$authHeader = Get-AuthorizationHeader -ClientId $clientId -RedirectUri $redirectUri\n$gateways = Get-Gateways -AuthorizationHeader $authHeader | Where-Object -Property \"name\" -EQ $gatewayName\nif($gateways.Count -eq 0) {\nWrite-EventLog -LogName \"Application\" -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -EventId 1000 -EntryType Error -Message \"Gateway '$gatewayName' does not exist.\"\n}\n$gateways | ForEach-Object {\n$gateway = Get-Gateway -GatewayID $_.id -AuthorizationHeader $authHeader\n$annotation = $gateway.gatewayAnnotation | ConvertFrom-Json\nif ($gateway.gatewayStatus -eq \"Live\") {\nWrite-EventLog -LogName \"Application\" -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -EventId 1000 -EntryType Information -Message \"Status of '$($gateway.name)' is '$($gateway.gatewayStatus)'.\nRunning on '$($annotation.gatewayMachine)'.\nVersion '$($annotation.gatewayVersion)'\"\n} else {\nWrite-EventLog -LogName \"Application\" -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -EventId 1000 -EntryType Error -Message \"Status of '$($gateway.name)' is '$($gateway.gatewayStatus)'.\nRunning on '$($annotation.gatewayMachine)'.\nVersion '$($annotation.gatewayVersion)'\"\n}\n}\n```","date":"2017-11-29"},{"url":"https://www.powershellgallery.com/packages/powerbi-powershell/1.20/Content/Samples%5CGet-GatewayStatus.ps1","title":"Samples/Get-GatewayStatus.ps1 1.20 - PowerShell Gallery","domain":"www.powershellgallery.com","summary":"### Samples/Get-GatewayStatus.ps1\n# RUN ONCE AS ADMINS# New-EventLog -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -LogName \"Application\"ca$clientId = \"\" # The ID of the Application ID of the App Registration in Azure AD\n$redirectUri = \"\" # One of the redirect URIs provided in the App Registration in Azure AD\n$gatewayName = \"\" # insert name of Gateway to check here.\n$authHeader = Get-AuthorizationHeader -ClientId $clientId -RedirectUri $redirectUri\n$gateways = Get-Gateways -AuthorizationHeader $authHeader | Where-Object -Property \"name\" -EQ $gatewayName\nif($gateways.Count -eq 0) {\nWrite-EventLog -LogName \"Application\" -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -EventId 1000 -EntryType Error -Message \"Gateway '$gatewayName' does not exist.\"\n}\n$gateways | ForEach-Object {\n$gateway = Get-Gateway -GatewayID $_.id -AuthorizationHeader $authHeader\n$annotation = $gateway.gatewayAnnotation | ConvertFrom-Json\nif ($gateway.gatewayStatus -eq \"Live\") {\nWrite-EventLog -LogName \"Application\" -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -EventId 1000 -EntryType Information -Message \"Status of '$($gateway.name)' is '$($gateway.gatewayStatus)'.\nRunning on '$($annotation.gatewayMachine)'.\nVersion '$($annotation.gatewayVersion)'\"\n} else {\nWrite-EventLog -LogName \"Application\" -Source \"On-premises data gateway Check\" -EventId 1000 -EntryType Error -Message \"Status of '$($gateway.name)' is '$($gateway.gatewayStatus)'.\nRunning on '$($annotation.gatewayMachine)'.\nVersion '$($annotation.gatewayVersion)'\"\n}\n}"},{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/gateway/overview?view=datagateway-ps","title":"PowerShell Cmdlets for On-premises data gateway management","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","date":"2025-06-05"},{"url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=H6sLCVanIYE","title":"Where are the Power BI Gateway logs? I thought I knew... - YouTube","domain":"www.youtube.com","summary":"Process Monitor\nhttps://learn.microsoft.com/sysinternals/downloads/procmon\nRui Romano's Gateway Monitor Report\nhttps://github.com/RuiRomano/pbigtwmonitor\n...\nThe first thing I know is\nthat when I want the logs, I go to diagnostics and I say,\nwell, gimme the gateway logs.\n{ts:31} And what this does is it actually exports the log files to a folder on your desktop, and it's a zip file, right?\n...\nThere is a tool out there from Sysinternals called Process Monitor.\n{ts:145} So you can get this up\non the Microsoft site.\nI'll have a link down in\nthe description below.\n...\n{ts:157} So I've had Process Monitor\nrunning in the background, and one thing you'll\nsee in the lower left is you can see this thing\nis just grabbing data.\n{ts:165} It's been running for a little bit.\nNow, one thing I'm\nalready pre-filtering is I'm saying don't include the registry,\n{ts:169} don't include network activity, don't include process and threat activity, and don't show profiling events.\n{ts:175} So I'm only looking at file\naccess here, all right?\nSo let's extract the files.\nAll right, there it is.\n{ts:181} And then let's stop the capture.\nAnd let's search for gateway.\nOh my gosh, sometimes searching\n...\nthat it needs to go after.\nSo let's do this instead.\nWe know it was towards the bottom.\n{ts:195} So what I'm looking at\nis this process name.\nI want to see, oh, there we go.\nSo I see EnterpriseGatewayConfigurator.\n{ts:201} So now what I can do is just go ahead, and let me say only\ninclude this process name.\nSo now we've just said\nignore everything else.\n{ts:210} I only wanna see the\nprocess name that's here.\nSo let's scroll down until we see something\nthat looks familiar.\n{ts:216} What I'm looking for\nhere is the gateway logs.\nAnd there we go.\nThat's the exported location.\n{ts:221} So I wanna know where is\nit actually getting for?\nAnd then we can see this item down below.\nAnd so here we can see it's\n{ts:227} C:\\Windows\\ServiceProfiles\\PBIEgwService.\nThat's where that service\naccount folder is.\nAnd then it's under\n\\AppData\\Local for that\n{ts:236} which is where you can\nfind that information.\nSuper cool.\n...\n{ts:241} saying, \"Hey, FYI, did you know this?\"\nAnd he came back and said, \"Well, if you're using Rui\nRomano's gateway monitor tool\n{ts:248} that he has out there, it's actually in the configuration file.\"\n...\n{ts:257} So if we go over on Rui Romano, so it's pbiegwmonitor, and\nthere's a config.json file which has the gateway path listed here.\n{ts:266} And so you could just quickly\nfigure it out from there.\nAnd if you end up","date":"2023-04-06"},{"url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/data-integration/gateway/service-gateway-tshoot","title":"Troubleshoot Gateway...","domain":"learn.microsoft.com","summary":"Connectivity issues can have several different causes.\nTherefore, if you run into any of the previously mentioned symptoms, perform the following verifications:\n1. Are the FQDNs and ports mentioned in our documentation opened/allowed in your firewall and/or proxy?\n2. If you're using a proxy server in your environment:\n1.\nMake sure the proxy server is properly configured in the gateway config files.\n2. Verify if the proxy configuration is consistent.\n3. Check your proxy logs to check if there are any requests being blocked at the proxy level.\n3. Is your Firewall just allowing the communication on ports 80 and 443?\n1. If yes, ensure the HTTPS mode in gateway is enabled.\n...\nThere are several logs you can collect for the gateway, and you should always start with the logs.\nThe simplest way to collect logs after you install the gateway is through the on-premises data gateway app.\nIn the on-premises data gateway app, select **Diagnostics** and then select the **Export logs** link.\nThis file is saved to the ODGLogs folder on your Windows desktop in .zip format.\n### Event logs\nTo find the event logs for the *on-premises data gateway service*, follow these steps:\n1. On the computer with the gateway installation, open the **Event Viewer**.\n2. Expand **Event Viewer** > **Applications and Services Logs**.\n3. Select **On-premises data gateway service**.","date":"2025-06-11"},{"url":"https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/how-to-capture-power-bi-on-premises-gateway-status-u-sai-teja-7jysc","title":"How-to-Capture-Power-BI-On-Premises-Gateway-Status-Using ...","domain":"www.linkedin.com","summary":"To capture the on-premises gateway status from Power BI Service, I've created a PowerShell script using Power REST API commands.\nThe script retrieves all the gateway details for which you have access, then iterates over those gateways to fetch the status, name, and other details.\nTo capture this data every hour, I've set up a Power Automate Desktop flow to execute the PowerShell script, and the output is stored in an Excel sheet on OneDrive.\nPlease check embedded github link for more details.","date":"2023-11-08"}],"error":null}],"total":8,"saved_to":"/home/user/.pplx/search/pplx_sdk_2026-06-30T23-34-45.751591Z_a3d9ab90.json"}
